---
/**
 * The second half of step 2: where the rental is collected from at the end.
 *
 * Rendered ONLY when something in the order is rented. A purchase has no second
 * half, and asking about a return the customer will never make is worse than not
 * asking.
 *
 * It sits BELOW the delivery methods rather than inside either panel, because the
 * question is the same one for both: a home delivery gets collected, a branch
 * collection gets brought back. Duplicating it into the two panels would mean two
 * checkboxes that must agree, and the copy is the only thing that differs — so the
 * copy is what changes, from `data-label-*`, and the control does not.
 *
 * The hairline above is the separation the step needed once it carried two
 * questions: delivery above, return below, each under its own eyebrow.
 */
import { t } from '~/lib/labels';
import { EYEBROW, FIELD_INPUT, FIELD_LABEL } from './styles';
---

<div class="border-hair mt-1.5 flex flex-col gap-3.5 border-t pt-5" data-return-block>
  <span class={EYEBROW}>{t('returnStage')}</span>

  {
    /*
      Checked by default, and server-rendered checked so it is already true before
      any script runs. Unticking it is the deliberate act, which is the right way
      round: almost every rental goes back where it came from.

      18px painted with `target-48` restoring the hit area — the deal described in
      the target-48 block of app.css. The two labels ride on the element as data
      attributes rather than being imported, for the same reason the delivery cards
      carry theirs: the page script must not pull in the label catalog.
    */
  }
  <label class="flex cursor-pointer items-center gap-3 text-[15px]">
    <input
      class="target-48 accent-accent size-4.5 flex-none cursor-pointer"
      type="checkbox"
      name="return-same-address"
      checked
      data-field-return-same
    />
    <span
      data-return-same-label
      data-label-home={t('returnSameAddress')}
      data-label-pickup={t('returnSameBranch')}>{t('returnSameAddress')}</span
    >
  </label>

  {
    /*
      One line, and deliberately not the comune/CAP ladder above. That ladder exists
      to key a PRICE and the return prices nothing — the fee was settled by the
      outbound comune. What is left is an address a driver reads, so the placeholder
      asks for the whole thing at once.
    */
  }
  <label class={FIELD_LABEL} data-return-address-field hidden>
    {t('returnAddressLabel')}
    <input
      class={FIELD_INPUT}
      type="text"
      name="return-address"
      autocomplete="off"
      placeholder={t('placeholderReturnAddress')}
      data-field="returnAddress"
    />
  </label>
</div>
